<h2>The prosecution case</h2>
<p>The appellant, aged 37 and the survivor's uncle by marriage, was alleged to have taken the 17-year-old from her village in Thanjavur district on 16 September 2014, kept her in hotel rooms in Chennai and Tiruppur, and committed repeated penetrative sexual assault before leaving her back at her village on 25 September 2014, when the FIR was lodged. The trial court (Fast Track Mahila Court, Thanjavur) convicted him in December 2016 under Sections 365 and 366 IPC and Section 6 of the POCSO Act, sentencing him to seven, ten and ten years respectively, concurrent.</p>
<p>The trial had one extraordinary feature: of fifteen prosecution witnesses, the defence cross-examined only the doctor (PW9). The survivor, her parents, her aunt and the investigating officers all went unchallenged.</p>

<h2>The remand plea — and why it failed</h2>
<p>On appeal, the defence sought a remand to cross-examine the uncross-examined witnesses. The Court refused, for three stacked reasons:</p>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Finality.</strong> The trial court dismissed a recall petition in September 2016; the High Court upheld that order in October 2016; neither was disturbed further. The question was closed.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Forfeited opportunity.</strong> The appellant had a fair opportunity to cross-examine and chose not to — an appellate remand is not a device to re-run a trial strategy that failed.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Child-witness protection.</strong> Section 33(5) POCSO obliges the Special Court to ensure the child is not called repeatedly to testify; recalling the survivor a decade later would defeat that mandate.</div>
</div>

<h2>Conviction, counts and sentence</h2>
<p>On the merits, the survivor's testimony — effectively unchallenged — was corroborated by the medical evidence (hymen not intact) and by her parents and aunt. Her age made consent immaterial, and the Court found the appellant's own account as DW1 not credible. The Section 6 POCSO conviction (through Section 5(l) — repeated assault) and the Section 366 IPC conviction stood.</p>
<p>The Court nevertheless corrected two trial-court errors. The Section 365 IPC conviction was set aside because it rested on the same act of abduction as Section 366 — one act cannot sustain two overlapping convictions. And the Section 366 sentence was reduced from ten years to three years rigorous imprisonment, the Court noting that the child was induced by a job pretext rather than seized by force. The ten-year POCSO sentence — the minimum under Section 6 as it stood before the 2019 amendment — remained, with concurrency and set-off under Section 428 CrPC.</p>
<table class="law">
