  <tr><td>Section 366 IPC</td><td>10 years RI</td><td>Upheld; sentence reduced to 3 years RI</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Section 6 POCSO</td><td>10 years RI</td><td>Upheld in full (statutory minimum pre-2019)</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Lessons for the defence and the prosecution</h2>
<p>For defence counsel, the case is a stark warning: cross-examination rights must be exercised at trial, and a recall application refused and affirmed is the end of the road. Appellate courts will scrutinise the record for prejudice, but they will not reward strategic silence with a retrial — least of all in POCSO cases, where Section 33(5) weighs against recalling the child. For the prosecution, the judgment shows that investigative lapses (the hotels were never traced) are not fatal where the survivor's account is consistent and corroborated. And for sentencing practice, it is a clean illustration of appellate courts enforcing the rule against double-counting overlapping IPC offences while respecting statutory minimums under special legislation.</p>
<div class="note">For occurrences after 1 July 2024, the corresponding IPC provisions are found in the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023 — kidnapping and abduction are dealt with under Sections 137 and 139-140 BNS — while the POCSO Act continues to apply as special law.</div>
